# A button plays notes on a buzzer — the example `buzzerTone` was specified
# for and never had.
#
#   button  P3.2  to GND, 10k pull-up to +5V    ACTIVE LOW
#   buzzer  P3.5  piezo between the pin and GND
#
# A tone is NOT PWM, and that is the whole point of this example. Every PWM
# path on this chip has a fixed carrier: clocking the PCA from Timer 0 gives
# 3.9 Hz, because Timer 0 is already the millisecond tick, and the hardware
# clock outputs divide an 8-bit reload and bottom out at 1800 Hz. So a tone is
# Timer 1 in 16-bit mode toggling the pin from its ISR, which reaches roughly
# 7 Hz upward and can therefore play an A4 at 440 Hz.
# See docs/STC12-PERIPHERAL-MODEL.md 5b.
#
# The board sees nothing new: the pin toggles, and `buzzerTone` in the board
# contract is DERIVED — the board measures the period on the buzzer's net and
# reports a frequency. Nothing in the MCU model knows about sound.
#
# Two things this costs, both deliberate. Timer 1 is consumed outright, so a
# program with a tone cannot also run under the debug monitor, which uses
# Timer 1 as the wall clock behind skew_ms. And the tone interrupt is given
# priority over the scheduler tick, because jitter here is audible rather than
# merely late.
#
# NOTE: unverified on silicon, like everything else here.

DEVICE STC12C5A60S2:
  CLOCK 11059200

  PIN button = P3.2 INPUT ACTIVE LOW     # PDIP-40 pin 12
  PIN buzzer = P3.5 TONE                 # PDIP-40 pin 15

  WHEN started:
    FOREVER:
      wait until button
      set buzzer to 440 hz
      wait 200 ms
      set buzzer to 554 hz
      wait 200 ms
      set buzzer to 659 hz
      wait 400 ms
      turn off buzzer
      wait until not button
